emotely the same hang up  while on the surface miesta's stand  ability seems to be sort of cartoonish  and somewhat limited what he ends up  contributing to this story is some of  the most entertaining portions of it  everything from humor to sadness to edge  of your seat action  araki constructed this story to feel as  though everyone and anyone is capable of  meeting their end at any time  and they almost do on so so many  occasions  unlike all the other acts in the story  where characters good and bad would be  either incapacitated and later learn  their lesson or wander off into  obscurity  characters here in this part die and not  just die  they get brutally murdered by the good  guys alleviated somewhat by the colorful  and light-hearted interactions they  share with each other  these death scenes are more brutal than  any i've come across in the past  employing in this section with mista  some overdone encounters like a  chase on a truck that has made all the  more fun utilizing stand powers making  the circumstances feel so fresh and  enjoyable ending in a clint eastwood  type  standoff with uh this guy getting shot  in the head  real good  [Music]  that's a lot of damage it soon becomes  obvious that there are a number of  competing gangs looking for the same  fortune that they are and once they find  it  they too come across a gentleman in  possession of their next mission  to deliver the boss's daughter home for  bruno  now the new capo and giorno this is  ideal giving them an excuse to get close  to the boss in some capacity and  assassinate him this is their once in a  lifetime opportunity  and their team doesn't need to even know  about it nancy  versus small man once the boss's  daughter her name is trish by the way  arrives on the scene in  inconspicuous clothing as a janitor she  soon chucks to get up and throws on her  ridiculous outfit you know  to match the rest of the main characters  so as i guess not to draw attention  to herself also she's super high  maintenance and so forces naranzia to go  out and grab some supplies before they  make their way to the next stop  [Music]  this is one of the coolest ideas for a  fight i've come across in a long while  and certainly within jojo while the plot  slowly progresses towards a satisfying  finish  i hope i really enjoyed the narrative  decision made by iraqi during these  combat sequences  instead of isolating the lifespan of  particular fights to the pages of a  couple chapters or in the case of prior  arcs one episode  this story allows the fight to breathe  and explore all the possibilities their  powers offer them in tandem with this  setting they find themselves in this  battle with narancia and the guy that  can make himself small  is honestly brilliant not just for  showcasing that ancient's cool and  powerful stand but also because of the  worsening circumstances this shrinking  man stand power can do if enough time  elapses  in essence if naranzia can put away this  guy fast his chances would only get  worse and worse as he slowly but surely  shrinks into his own defeat  and let me tell you they do everything  with this premise with the elements used  in the ultimate defeat  of this shrinking men being equally  engaging and satisfying a lot of the  dialogue in prior fights would consist  of  immediate concerns brought forth by the  present battle however much of these  dialogue exchanges in golden wooden  concern themselves primarily with  motivations linked directly to the  broader story while also serving as a  great launching off point to inform us  the audience about the backstory of  characters like  to help build a bond with him and in the  case of the shrinking man we get to see  some of his past too  through which we learn about his  extended team of stand users on the  trail of bruno and giorno's team  he returns back to base and discovers  that bruno has received an email  directly from the boss  they need to go to the archaeological  park where there's a hidden key for a  vehicle that they can use to bring his  daughter home  they split the team one half with giorno  goes to get the key the other half with  bruno stays here and guards the daughter  of the boss  the archaeological park our time spent  at this archaeological park once again  gives us an opportunity to get to know a  new member of the squad all the while  building a new bond between the  characters  on screen and progressing the plot  during this section we come to learn  more about fugo with an iq of 152 he  managed to get into university  remarkably early however was kicked out  after he attacked a professor however as  has been the case with almost all of  these backstories including journals the  initial description of their past  doesn't seem to match up with how the  events  actually measured up when explored these  are all victims in some shape or form  with fugal particularly falling into  that category helping to explain how and  why he is so detached and aggressive  so much of the time the battle that  takes place is awesome with a focus of  course following fugo as he gets trapped  inside the mirror dimension  where have i seen that before but both  giorno and leon get some minor shine  during this outing too  as i'm editing this video it literally  just occurred to me that he's been  saying leone wrong the entire time  which is really funny because he only  started saying that first name because  he was too scared to say abakio i give  up  i give up i give up i've tried i tried  i'm sorry  and thanks to their combined effort and  some jaw-droppingly clever thinking on  behalf of jorno fugo's ridiculously  powerful stand gets a target to take out  however what was brought to my attention  during this section wasn't necessarily  all of that though it was knocking  around in there but what i really was  thinking about was how little of giorno  i've gotten to see  during this part of the story once the  setup in the initial episodes wrap up  journal feels very much like a stand-in  for the audience at times allowing for  exposition to be explained which again  is vital but  also feels like he's very much in  service equal part to the side  characters making the entire team  themselves feeling less like side  characters and more like main characters  and no  section made me feel this more than what  comes next after they discover the key  and the vehicle that it fits into battle  on the train  of the three major battles before this  climax i had to say that this is  easily my favorite part of the first  half and battled therein and it's not  even  remotely close with the others the level  of creativity and fun araki was clearly  having during this section  oozes from every frame and manga panel  taking place over the course of three  entire episodes the gang arrive at the  train station followed through the point  of view of the villains we saw in the  flashback earlier through small man  during this encounter araki spends  almost equal amounts of time with the  good guys and the bad guys making it  throughout feel as though any side could  potentially fail or come away with a win  here and the funny part is that giorno  barely participates with the rocky  instead opting to make full use of mista  and bruno's characters during this fight  sequence and  it's all the better for it when i say  that this fight has  everything i mean it there's some  mystery in the bad guys trying to  discover where the good guys have gone  to in the train they're in a tortoise by  the way there's some emotional  development for a character of pesci  that undergoes his own character arc  within these episodes there's a sense of  mourning on both sides after they think  that they've lost their respective  partners there's ludicrously engaging  action with some of the most creative  implementation of stand abilities in  this series  and it elevates every character directly  involved so much so that the two  characters of mista and bruno became my  two  favorite characters within this part of  the story elevating them both above  giorno for me  and that's just the mechanics within  this fight araki makes full use of the  location that this takes place in two  they kill a bunch of people trapped in a  train they fight on the roof they fight  on the sides they fight on the inside  and they even stop the train and finish  it outside  at numerous instances during this  encounter araki made full use of what  the audience was aware of  and what the characters weren't to help  build tension also  and he did this with both sides for  instance when one of the two brothers  starts to use his stand to age people up  we watch on as bruno's team try to piece  together what's going on  and watch them try to figure out how to  stop it conversely with the other side  of the action towards the very end bruno  needs to make full use of his stand  power in the most  incredible and literally  heart-stoppingly intense way he can  all the while we watch on as the fishing  rod wielding pesci struggles to figure  out where he went i could talk about  this section of the story and this  battle specifically for ages but in the  interest of time  these if nothing else are the episodes i  think that will sell you on part 5  if you are not a fan of it yet it's  honestly incredible and the best by far  in the series  delivering trish to her father i'm not  the biggest fan  of the next conflict that largely  concerns giorno not because i don't like  gerna but because the battle itself felt  sort of  small and anticlimactic perhaps because  it came immediately after my favorite  part of the story so  let's jump ahead to venice instead  there's another terrific section of the  story focusing on the ice battle with  jorno and mister that absolutely  solidified mister as my second favorite  character in the story it has some  incredible animation and let me just  tell you this entire part that i've  watched so far  is top to bottom gorgeous with some  truly brilliant animated moments  on the topic of mista i adore his  personality and his tenacity during this  combat sequence and his little sex  pistols are  always a treat the drama in this fight  is fittingly high and the action  provided was  thrilling probably my second favorite  fight along with narancia's fight  earlier  also it might very well have the best  ending of all the fights i've seen  [Music]  one rather important critique i have for  this section however is  perhaps how it handled the character of  trish as the daughter of the boss and  the package that they've had to deliver  over the course of this story the only  thing  she's really contributed to it have been  some small few  snarky comments as well as some comedic  fanservice during the midpoint which i  never really enjoy it's not my thing  given how much time has been spent with  this team i would have liked to have  cared a bit more about trish  at all by the time she ultimately got  delivered to her father  as she's taken up the elevator to meet  her long-lost familial patriarch she  finally starts to show glimpses of  character and uncertainty through the  rough and brash exterior she's putting  up throughout this trip  this is great but what would have been  much more effective is having this sort  of exposition earlier on in the trip  that way tension could potentially build  more effectively on their way to the  tower and heck maybe it might even give  more of a motivation to bruno maybe he  could have been the one to talk her into  it and once things inevitably go awry  once they get to the island in venice  there could have been more of a reason  for him to blame himself and take  personal responsibility for her capture  and possible death i thought trish  offered the opportunity to be a rich  character with tons to offer the  narrative in order to up the stakes  however instead she came across much  more like a damsel in distress or  possibly more accurately a mcguffin or  plot device used to forward the  narrative to the midpoint climax like  she ultimately did  i hope that by the time i get around to  the rest of the second half she plays a  much larger role that offers more to the  story than a mute animal could otherwise  have achieved one thing  i want to say also about the big bad of  this arc is that while i appreciate they  wanted to create an insurmountable or  intimidating force to overcome  using their powers in creative ways i  sort of feel as though araki has gone to  the time manipulation well  one too many times during these stories  i mean since stands have been introduced  the big bad of the ark has  always had on some level time  manipulation abilities  and after giving it some thought i've  grown to understand why  to some extent it's incredibly powerful  realistically when you're creating a  villain there are three schools of  thought i think  firstly there's making your antagonist  impossibly powerful like dio in some  respects and certainly like the boss  from this part 5.  the goal of these villains is not to be  relatable or likable but instead to  provide a wall for which  the good guys are to overcome secondly  there's the opposite of what i just  demonstrated a villain to be and that's  being weaker than the hero only to try  his best to get more powerful and  eventually become that threat  these first two are the most common and  to be honest both have their ups and  down sides characters like dio can feel  less like characters and more like walls  of power to overcome  furthermore they also have a tendency to  get predictable and sort of boring if  you over rely on that archetype too  often  the weaker villain has the opposite  problem in that they are too relatable  people in general love to cheer for the  underdog tenaciously fighting against  all odds and so putting your protagonist  into a more powerful position  prior means you need to make certain  that your hero is incredibly likable to  your reader or viewer in order for them  to cheer for them  alternatively then there's the third  option which iraqi decided to opt for  during diamond is unbreakable story with  kira and kira ended up being my favorite  antagonist out of this series  instead of having a villain that is  obviously weaker or stronger than josuke  he instead decided to make him a  compliment to josuke sort of like the  other side of a coin if you will i  thought kira was a brilliant foil for  josuke and the most ideal candidate  among the jojo villains for that very  reason and he didn't need time powers  either  proven by him not using them once during  his fight with josuke and only using  them in the lead up to the battle  now obviously there are ways of screwing  that option up too seen all too well in  marvel films where the superheroes like  iron man captain america ant-man doctor  strange and black panther even  all seem to face off against evil  versions of themselves  instead of flipping the power set they  flip the personality behind the power  which isn't always bad but over the  course of a series can become formulaic  which i think is jojo's problem right  now with this time manipulation ability  while i did love this scene with bruno  and giorno in the gloomy hideout  confronting the big boss for the very  first time  i couldn't help but feel a little  underwhelmed when i discovered that ooh  look hits time powers again when the  powers of this dude exist that could  have served as a much more of an  interesting match-up against  jordano's power specifically that we  never really got to see  jordan can give life and he can  technically take it away  now that is all obviously hinging on the  idea that giorno is the main character  and judging by the way this is going it  seems to be much more of an ensemble  cast  rather than a particularly focused  protagonistic story so we'll see how  things go  anyways and so now with this big boss  man after putt